Type: Friction
What Changed: SaaS operators already paying for Stripe Billing (including Smart Retries) encounter friction where hard declines such as expired_card and authentication_required continue to trigger scheduled retries that cannot succeed without customer intervention. Observed in primary discussion around 2026-11-23 to 2026-11-24.
Friction: Payment recovery and dunning layer for non-retryable decline codes; default retry schedule proceeds without immediate optimized customer action path.
Money: Businesses pay Stripe processing/Billing fees and often additional recovery tools or engineering time; still must manually implement webhook logic or custom dunning to handle hard declines separately. Associated with revenue loss via unrecovered involuntary churn (industry benchmarks indicate 20-40% of total SaaS churn is involuntary from failed payments). Exact per-business amounts unknown.
Evidence:
1. Reddit r/stripe post (2026-11-23): User reviewing webhook logs notes Stripe Smart Retries continue 3-4 attempts on expired_card (which cannot succeed) and authentication_required (requires customer present for 3DS). Asks how others structure listeners to suppress or escalate immediately. URL: https://www.reddit.com/r/stripe/comments/1y3t4lf/why_do_stripe_smart_retries_keep_reattempting/
2. Supporting analyses (2026): Multiple sources (e.g., Baremetrics, Rebounce, DunnAI) confirm retries do not recover expired_card, lost/stolen, or authentication_required; require customer update or authentication flow. Card updater covers some but not all cases.
3. Benchmark context: Recurly July 2026: involuntary churn ~1.06% for SaaS; 20-40% of total churn attributed to failed payments across sources.
Mechanism: Uniform default retry logic treats hard declines like soft ones, generating failed attempts, delaying customer notification, and reducing recovery window before subscription cancels. Inferred from platform behavior and user reports.
Current Workaround: Custom webhook handlers on invoice.payment_failed to detect specific decline codes, suppress further retries, and trigger targeted dunning (email/in-app with update link). Cost is engineering effort (unknown quantified hours per team); some use third-party recovery tools.
Payer: SaaS founders and operators using Stripe for subscriptions, evidenced by purchase of dedicated dunning/recovery tools (e.g., Churnkey starting ~$250/mo) and ongoing custom development discussions.
Economic Effect: revenue (lost MRR from unrecovered payments), labor (custom code and monitoring), failed transaction.
Friction Value: Frequency: recurring for any subscription business with card payments (exact monthly failed rate unknown, often 5-10% of renewals per sources). Single event impact: lost ARPU for that period if unrecovered. Recoverable rate: higher with prompt customer action (tools report 30-50%+ recovery on addressable failures); specific for hard declines lower without intervention. Numbers approximate from industry reports; per-instance unknown.
Persistence: Structural. Card network decline codes and platform default retry behaviors are longstanding; not a temporary bug.
